Title: smoke_upstream reports a cold run as warm and undercounts RRF scores
Created: 2026-09-03
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-09-03T12:07Z claude created

--- body ---

## Context

Found while re-running `bench/smoke_upstream.py` at the v1.13.0 baseline
(ticket 0618). Two reporting defects, neither of which changes a verdict, and
both of which put a wrong number in a committed artifact. Filed apart from the
re-baseline because a measurement-reporting fix must not ride a pin move.

**The warm figure keeps the cold run.** The script sorts the per-query wall
times and drops the first element, which is the *minimum*, not the cold run.
The first query pays the model download and the ONNX session start; on a fresh
data directory that is the largest number in the set and it survives into a
field labelled as if the cold run had been excluded, while the fastest warm
query is the one thrown away.

**The RRF counter reads zero where every score is exactly the RRF value.** The
check compares the returned scores against a contiguous prefix of the
rank-fusion series. Item-level deduplication in the query path leaves gaps in
the ranks, so a query whose hits skip a rank fails strict prefix equality even
though every individual score is exactly the fusion value for its own rank. At
the previous baseline each query returned at most one hit, so contiguity held
trivially and the counter read three; the number moved for a reason that is the
check's, not the target's. The substantive claim the field supports — that the
score is a relabelled rank carrying no similarity magnitude — is unaffected.

## Actions

1. Compare each score against the fusion value for *its own* rank, not against
   a contiguous prefix. State in the artifact how many hits were compared.
2. Report the cold run as its own field and compute the warm figure from what
   remains, or say plainly that the field includes the first query.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] Both fields are computed from what the run observed and labelled with
      what they include
- [ ] A run on a fresh data directory and a run on a warm one produce fields a
      reader can tell apart
